WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.760
Going into this show I thought it could have&nbsp;
some potential, but I ended up leaving it mostly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:09.680
disappointed and wanting more. Echo is kind of a&nbsp;
fresh slate character that I didn’t know anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:14.840
about, so I thought this could be an interesting&nbsp;
mini-series. Especially since one of the writers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:19.640
from Better Call Saul was working on the show and&nbsp;
Marvel was going to finally dive into something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:24.440
that was more mature. But if anything, I feel&nbsp;
like the R-rated marketing campaign was more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:29.640
of a way to get people’s attention because it&nbsp;
was still pretty PG-13. And the writing was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:34.480
nothing special. Which going into the first&nbsp;
major shortfall is that Echo as a miniseries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:39.720
is very underdeveloped. The creators try to do a&nbsp;
lot with a pretty short runtime because the show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:45.400
is only 3 hours once you subtract the opening&nbsp;
intros and end-credits. So in this time they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:50.400
try to tackle a family drama with a handful of&nbsp;
characters, an entire backstory and re-cap of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:55.440
Hawkeye that spans the first episode, a more&nbsp;
detailed relationship between Maya and Fisk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.440 --> 00:01:01.760
this native american power origin, and then the&nbsp;
entire war/conflict between Maya and Fisk. I’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:06.720
seen people say that the show should have been&nbsp;
cut down to like 2 hours, but I’d actually argue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:12.360
the opposite. Echo needed a much longer runtime to&nbsp;
properly develop all of these different aspects of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:18.080
the show. Because by being stretched so thin and&nbsp;
trying to cover so much, it in turn isn’t able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:23.960
to properly build-up these elements and pay them&nbsp;
off in a satisfying manner. Like as an example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:29.440
Maya gets ripped away from most of her family as&nbsp;
a child and then she returns to her hometown. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:34.560
I think is an interesting dynamic to build upon&nbsp;
relationships, but the show is preoccupied with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:38.920
so much that the character work never hits a&nbsp;
satisfying depth. And you probably wouldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:44.040
be expecting a family drama out of Echo, but&nbsp;
I honestly found some of it compelling. Mainly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:48.200
because the first episode of the show makes you&nbsp;
sympathize and care about Maya and you want her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:53.720
to rekindle her past relationships and thus become&nbsp;
happier in life. The parallel between Fisk being a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:58.640
manipulative figure and a toxic influence versus&nbsp;
her Native American family that wants to truly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:03.920
love her I thought was pretty good. Along with&nbsp;
the acting requiring more physicality and facial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:09.040
expressions since Maya was deaf and characters&nbsp;
interacting with her had to use sign language.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:13.920
If anything, I thought the whole action side of&nbsp;
the show was very underwhelming and was in general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:19.000
unimaginative. This may be a major turn off for&nbsp;
most of you because Marvel as a brand is supposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:23.920
to be an exciting and action packed adventure,&nbsp;
but that isn’t what Echo is. They try to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:29.240
it a more grounded drama series with a strong&nbsp;
native american perspective. There’s action here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:34.280
but it’s definitely not the selling point of&nbsp;
the series. Which I think is kind of refreshing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:38.880
but again, since the series is so short and is&nbsp;
stretched so thin, I don’t think it’s able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:43.680
hit a high enough quality to really be worth the&nbsp;
time. Like if you really wanted a great recent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:49.200
native american story then go watch Killers of&nbsp;
the Flower Moon. It’s basically the same runtime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:54.840
and that movie is way better. To not purely bash&nbsp;
the show, I think the performances in Echo are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:59.440
pretty solid and it’s mostly unfortunate that the&nbsp;
writers couldn’t give these characters more scenes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:04.560
to flesh out their dynamics. Hell, Maya’s best&nbsp;
friend has like one quick and awkward scene with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:08.960
Maya and she doesn’t get any kind of emotional&nbsp;
reconnection with Maya in the end. It feels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:13.560
so rushed and unfulfilling as a dramatic story&nbsp;
that it makes me wonder if the writer actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:17.600
wrote it this way, or if they were pressured&nbsp;
by the producers to make the story shorter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:22.160
or if there were some budgetary issues. Like&nbsp;
the premise I think is pretty solid and should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:27.800
allow for a great story and with more time and&nbsp;
episodes, I assume that could have been the case.

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:33.080
Now going into the other more inferior half of the&nbsp;
show in my opinion, let’s talk about the Fisk and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:38.800
overall war plotline. There’s a lot that annoys me&nbsp;
and first, let’s discuss the relationship between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:43.680
Fisk and Maya because it largely feels like a&nbsp;
retcon. You’re telling me that this character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:50.040
who Fisk has been raising, is very close to him,&nbsp;
and is a deadly fighter that he regularly uses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:56.040
never went up against Daredevil during his show? I&nbsp;
know Echo as an IP wasn’t even on Netflix’s radar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.040 --> 00:04:00.600
when they were making Daredevil, but supplanting&nbsp;
her into that narrative when it doesn’t make any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:06.120
sense I think is pretty jarring. Like Fisk was&nbsp;
on the ropes fighting for his life using all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:12.200
his assets during that show and apparently Maya&nbsp;
was never used. She’s just been hiding in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:17.920
background until now. So that’s kind of an aspect&nbsp;
that you have to accept about the story because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:23.360
the writers want to make Fisk an integral part of&nbsp;
Maya’s life. When that was never set up before.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:28.760
From here, the relationship between Maya and Fisk&nbsp;
feels rather inconsistent and confuses me. At&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:33.840
the end of Hawkeye, she flat-out murders Fisk by&nbsp;
shooting him in the face and I was like wow what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:38.880
an underwhelming way to end such a magnificent&nbsp;
villain in Daredevil. And then after the show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:43.920
they said Fisk would return in Echo and was like&nbsp;
Huhhh? I was eagerly awaiting the explanation as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:49.280
to how Fisk would survive a gunshot wound to&nbsp;
the head and apparently all they did was slap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:54.560
a bandage onto his eye like Phil Swift plugging a&nbsp;
hole of water with Flex tape. It’s such a joke of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:58.880
a way to handle this character and why would&nbsp;
you ever write it to where Fisk gets shot in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:04.520
the head and returns. That completely undercuts&nbsp;
any stakes and weight for death scenes because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:09.080
if Fisk can survive a headshot wound then I’m not&nbsp;
going to be concerned about his well-being in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:13.880
future. It’s just a mind-boggling writing choice&nbsp;
that feels the same as Sabine getting skewered by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:19.400
a lightsaber. But at least she didn’t sustain a&nbsp;
critical injury where her brain was. From here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:24.720
most of the confrontations between Fisk and Maya&nbsp;
are him approaching her with no defenses as she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:30.280
has a gun trained on him. Which seems confusing&nbsp;
to me because if she was so eager to kill him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:35.800
then why not do so again. I know Fisk is trying&nbsp;
to manipulate her, but most of their shared scenes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:41.120
together he fails to do so and she for some reason&nbsp;
chooses to not kill him. Despite her not really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:45.800
changing much since her first attempt at trying&nbsp;
to take his life. She does change by the end of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:50.960
the show and without going into spoilers she has a&nbsp;
different solution to Fisk as a problem which just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:55.960
felt really weird. It has to do with her entire&nbsp;
power which I also thought wasn’t explained that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.960 --> 00:06:00.360
much. They left it relatively vague and I’m not&nbsp;
sure if that was their intention because it seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:05.080
to give her multiple unrelated powers. But I&nbsp;
feel like going into that would be a spoiler,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:10.280
so I won’t do that. Overall, the dynamic between&nbsp;
Fisk and Maya was not convincing because the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:15.560
writers primarily tell us that they have this&nbsp;
strong bond without really establishing it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.560 --> 00:06:20.280
Like why is Fisk so obsessed with Maya when&nbsp;
he has so much more on his plate? What about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:25.760
her does he love so much? Why is he going so far&nbsp;
out of his way to be this father figure and raise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:30.920
her? The only real motivation they establish&nbsp;
is that Fisk was mostly using her as a weapon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.920 --> 00:06:35.720
but Fisk has plenty of those. And why would he&nbsp;
risk killing her father when there’s a possibility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:40.560
she could find out. To me, it all comes across as&nbsp;
forcing Maya and Fisk together to try and make an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:46.200
interesting story without it seeming realistic.&nbsp;
Especially since she’s non-existent in Daredevil.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:50.480
Which I could honestly forgive that if they&nbsp;
actually dedicated more time to establishing her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:56.120
past with Fisk and answering all those questions,&nbsp;
but again, that means more time for telling this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.120 --> 00:07:01.560
story. Leading back to the issue of Echo largely&nbsp;
being underdeveloped. Also within this half of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:06.440
show we have the action side of things. I’ve seen&nbsp;
some people say that they really liked it, but to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:11.760
me, I thought it was pretty flawed. It has some&nbsp;
great choreography, however, I think it’s dragged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:16.400
down by some of its cinematography and editing.&nbsp;
The first action scene with Daredevil uses the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:21.480
notorious fake oner that if done poorly can be&nbsp;
really distracting and can also be a detriment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:25.960
to the presentation of the action. Because what’s&nbsp;
even the point of faking a oner if you’re just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.960 --> 00:07:31.120
going to wipe the camera and hide cuts every 3&nbsp;
seconds. It’s painfully obvious and since they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:36.000
are trying to create one continuous shot, it leads&nbsp;
to some moments being awkwardly framed. There have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:40.640
been some incredible examples of fake oners like&nbsp;
in Guardians of the Galaxy 3 where the hidden cuts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:45.840
aren’t as jarring and the action is always framed&nbsp;
in a great way. Or you have Daredevil where they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:51.480
actually do real oners. So this action scene while&nbsp;
it does have some great choreography, I think is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:56.782
filmed in a bad way. Then for another fight&nbsp;
scene, the editing was very sporadic and fast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.782 --> 00:08:01.800
paced making the edit overly jarring. That and&nbsp;
they would also try to draw parallels to Maya’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:07.600
Native American past by using quick cutaways of&nbsp;
her ancestors fighting. Which makes the edit even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:12.200
more chaotic and annoying. It’s also really&nbsp;
sad because every now and then they’ll have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:17.600
wide shot longer take showing the action, meaning&nbsp;
that this is the level they could be operating on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:22.240
but then they butcher it with a bunch of quick&nbsp;
unnecessary cuts. And the editor even utilizes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:27.080
jump cuts for this one shot. There’s just so many&nbsp;
editing choices that don’t feel well-suited for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.080 --> 00:08:32.240
the action. Then for an action scene at night,&nbsp;
the editor raised the brightness so much to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:36.560
the point that the black levels looked terrible.&nbsp;
Like why would you even bother making this scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:41.080
outside at night if you were going to butcher&nbsp;
the look of your scene. Not to mention that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.080 --> 00:08:46.440
action scene was overall very unimaginative and&nbsp;
boring. It was just Maya running across a train,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.440 --> 00:08:50.800
planting something, and then she jumps off. Like&nbsp;
the writers really couldn’t come up with anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:56.320
more interesting or engaging? Also in these action&nbsp;
scenes, they come across as having barely any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.320 --> 00:09:02.200
impact or consequences because Maya always comes&nbsp;
away unscathed. She deadass throws her body off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.200 --> 00:09:08.200
of a train and gets rocked by a truck that then&nbsp;
hard brakes and she’s A OK. She even has this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:13.080
massive bullet wound in the side of her torso that&nbsp;
gets sowed up and then literally the scene after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.080 --> 00:09:19.440
this she climbs an entire water tower. Like Maya&nbsp;
as a character sustains no physical punishments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:25.120
that made a show like Daredevil feel so raw and&nbsp;
real. The only thing of consequence that happens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:30.720
to Maya is that her prosthetic leg gets crushed,&nbsp;
but it instantly gets replaced. The fight scenes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:36.440
in Daredevil however were deeply investing because&nbsp;
we knew that Matt Murdoch was always in danger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.440 --> 00:09:40.760
because he would sustain life threatening&nbsp;
injuries. But in Echo? The writers couldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:45.560
be bothered with doing this because I guess they&nbsp;
don’t have enough time to do so. So that in turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.560 --> 00:09:50.720
makes the action feel weightless. They just throw&nbsp;
some flashy action around and move on instantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.720 --> 00:09:56.120
as if nothing happened. Another main reason why&nbsp;
Daredevil was so great is that they balanced the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.120 --> 00:10:00.920
action downtime with deep and personal character&nbsp;
development. This is what Echo should have been,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.920 --> 00:10:06.440
and wasn’t. There’s only like a few action scenes&nbsp;
and maybe a couple emotional dialogue scenes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.440 --> 00:10:10.720
Also, Maya literally shows up to her hometown&nbsp;
and jeopardizes everyone’s safety by bringing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.720 --> 00:10:16.520
about a war because of her own personal vendetta.&nbsp;
She’s risking the lives of her entire family every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.520 --> 00:10:21.360
step of the way and she’s even called out on&nbsp;
this, but this entire notion is dropped by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:27.160
end of the show and is not explored again. There&nbsp;
are basically no consequences for her actions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.160 --> 00:10:31.720
They set up like six characters in her family&nbsp;
and none of them die. Which I don’t need there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.720 --> 00:10:36.520
to be character deaths, but instead tangible&nbsp;
consequences. This in turn makes the story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.520 --> 00:10:41.600
lack depth because the writers are acting like&nbsp;
this critical conflict doesn’t exist. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.600 --> 00:10:46.040
they should be exploring this considerably and&nbsp;
even have Maya as a character confront the fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.040 --> 00:10:50.960
that what she’s doing is wrong. Thus changing&nbsp;
and evolving her as a character. And the way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.960 --> 00:10:55.840
she changes is through her rekindling her love&nbsp;
for her true family. Then the general plot for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.840 --> 00:11:00.640
the overall war if you can even call it that&nbsp;
is so stereotypical and nothing happens in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.640 --> 00:11:06.120
story that is remotely interesting. Maya just&nbsp;
attacks a compound and Fisk realiates briefly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.120 --> 00:11:10.600
and that’s basically it in terms of the plot.&nbsp;
There’s not a single surprise in the entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.600 --> 00:11:15.800
story and it’s probably one of the most generic&nbsp;
plots that Marvel ever created. Along with a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.800 --> 00:11:20.200
of scenes being filled with dumb moments like&nbsp;
Maya getting captured and one of her kidnappers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.200 --> 00:11:25.840
kindly giving back her foot, which she uses to&nbsp;
get out. Maya also randomly becomes Macgiver in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.840 --> 00:11:30.240
one scene and makes this complicated invention&nbsp;
despite this skill never being established,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.240 --> 00:11:34.480
or Fisk conveniently calling off his men&nbsp;
right before they’re about to kill Maya.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.480 --> 00:11:40.120
And heyyy, these all take place in one episode.&nbsp;
So this entire war aspect of the story isn’t just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.120 --> 00:11:45.200
dull, but it’s also pretty dumb. That’s why I’d&nbsp;
say the actual meat of this story is the family&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:50.200
drama which will inherently disappoint some&nbsp;
people and turn others away. It does have some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.200 --> 00:11:54.440
strong moments like the scene between Maya and her&nbsp;
grandmother which was infused with an endearing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.440 --> 00:11:59.640
emotional connection, but these moments are few&nbsp;
and far between and aren’t enough to redeem the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.640 --> 00:12:05.080
utter mediocrity of Echo. I don’t think Echo as a&nbsp;
show is as bad as Secret Invasion, but I wouldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.080 --> 00:12:10.520
be surprised if it gets overrated by Marvel fans&nbsp;
purely because it isn’t an abomination. From my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.520 --> 00:12:14.600
perspective, I don’t think this show is worth the&nbsp;
time because there’s so many other better shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.600 --> 00:12:18.880
and movies to watch. Thank you for watching and if&nbsp;
you want to see more of my ratings since I don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.880 --> 00:12:23.760
do videos on everything I watch then check out my&nbsp;
Letterboxd. And my Patreon and discord links are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.760 --> 00:12:28.880
in the description. Have a great day and let me&nbsp;
know what you thought about Echo in the comments.

